{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghx7L33n8ii9Bo9qHSCy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SUvGLpGFWh2Hv9PJ95ep63","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-build--20261104-061612--x5a4f79-work/213edd46-9651-46f8-a282-da1d89430eab/tool-results/blpovl870.txt","limit":400},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1013,"cache_read_input_tokens":22185,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1013},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"213edd46-9651-46f8-a282-da1d89430eab","uuid":"c3e9cfda-09ff-4211-baaa-7a941a5374cc","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:16:23.079Z","request_id":"req_011Cghx7KjCEdPfrD6tGayMg","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01SUvGLpGFWh2Hv9PJ95ep63":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-build--20261104-061612--x5a4f79-work/213edd46-9651-46f8-a282-da1d89430eab/tool-results/blpovl870.txt","limit":400}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SUvGLpGFWh2Hv9PJ95ep63","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# BASKETBLAM\n2\t\n3\t**Over-the-top 2-on-2 arcade basketball. No fouls. No mercy. All BLAM.**\n4\t\n5\tThis is the build plan for the demo TASK.md describes. It is written for the developers (people or AI models) who will\n6\tbuild the game from TASK.md and this file alone, without the planner. Read TASK.md first, then this file once, top to\n7\tbottom; after that use it as a reference. Everything here is a decision, not a menu: where a number is given, start\n8\twith it and tune it with the dev sliders (§14). Where this file and TASK.md disagree, TASK.md wins.\n9\t\n10\t---\n11\t\n12\t## 0. At a glance\n13\t\n14\t| | |\n15\t|---|---|\n16\t| Title | **BASKETBLAM** (on screen); `Basketblam` in file names |\n17\t| Genre | Arcade sports: 2-on-2 basketball in the over-the-top arcade style, with a short story ladder |\n18\t| Perspective | Side-on **broadcast camera**: a custom orthographic view, pitch 30°, the court running left to right, panning with the ball; a dunk cam that zooms; a \"High\" camera option |\n19\t| Players | 1–4 on one machine (gamepads and a shared keyboard); 1–4 online in AI Hub sessions (shared world) |\n20\t| Modes | Blam Tour (story, 4 games), Exhibition (any teams and courts, 1–4 players), Blam Camp (interactive tutorial), Hall of Blam (records), How to Play, Options, About/Credits; attract mode; dev mode; AI Hub \"Showdown\" |\n21\t| Content | 5 courts, each with its own twist; 6 teams (4 street crews, a robot boss team, a secret team), 18 players and 5 story characters; 12 dunks; 6 power-ups; 10 tutorial lessons; 15 story beats; ~200 announcer lines; 12 songs and 8 stings; about 55 sound effects; 21 voice profiles |\n22\t| Tech | my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, our modified copy), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript, Canvas 2D, Web Audio; Node 24 + Playwright 1.63 for tests |\n23\t| Deliverable | one self-contained HTML file in the root, plus `exit-interview.json` beside it |\n24\t\n25\t**The core loop, in one sentence:** get the ball, beat your defender with turbo, spin moves and passes, and finish with a\n26\tdunk that leaves the atmosphere or a jumper released at the top of the jump; on defense swipe, swat and shove (it's\n27\tlegal); score three straight and you go **SUPERNOVA**.\n28\t\n29\t**Pillars** (use them to settle any question this plan doesn't):\n30\t1. **Every possession can end in spectacle.** Dunks, blocks, shoves and steals are big, readable and loud.\n31\t2. **Simple buttons, real timing.** Four main buttons; skill lives in release timing, turbo management, alley-oop\n32\t   timing and steal angles.\n33\t3. **Personality everywhere.** Two hype announcers, trash talk, courts with twists, a crowd that reacts.\n34\t4. **Couch first, 10 feet away.** Up to four on one screen, a TV-readable HUD, controller-first menus.\n35\t\n36\t---\n37\t\n38\t## 1. Title, names and folders\n39\t\n40\t- **Title: BASKETBLAM.** Picked as TASK.md asks: fifty riffs on \"basketball\" were brainstormed and one was chosen with a\n41\t  real random number generator (Python `secrets.randbelow(50)` returned 18). Do not re-pick it.\n42\t- **SOURCE token** in file names: `Basketball`.\n43\t- **Names for this TASK.md** (copy them exactly):\n44\t  - dev folder: `Basketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174822/` (in the root, beside TASK.md)\n45\t  - deliverable: `Basketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174822.html` (in the root)\n46\t  - exit interview: `exit-interview.json` (in the root, beside the HTML)\n47\t\n48\t  If your TASK.md names another model or timestamp, use your TASK.md's names with the same `Basketblam` and\n49\t  `Basketball` tokens.\n50\t- **Dev folder layout:**\n51\t\n52\t```\n53\tBasketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174822/\n54\t  handoff/            pristine copy of my-3d2dge dist/handoff (docs, tools/check.mjs, filmstrip.mjs, asset tools)\n55\t  engine/my-3d2dge.js our modified engine (every change marked /* BASKETBLAM: ... */)\n56\t  vendor/aihub-session.js       AI Hub client 1.4.0, byte for byte\n57\t  vendor/hub-harness/           harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (tests only, never bundled)\n58\t  src/*.js            the game, as numbered fragments (§4.2)\n59\t  tools/build.mjs     concatenates src into build/game.js, writes build/index.html and the root deliverable\n60\t  tests/run.mjs       the one test command (§19); tests/*.test.mjs; tests/helpers.mjs; tests/fakehub.mjs\n61\t  build/              generated: game.js, index.html (dev page), hub/ (served for harness tests)\n62\t  check-output/       screenshots and filmstrips for review (generated)\n63\t  package.json        { \"type\": \"module\", devDependencies: { \"playwright\": \"1.63.0\" } }\n64\t```\n65\t\n66\t---\n67\t\n68\t## 2. Genre, perspective and look\n69\t\n70\t**Genre: arcade sports, 2-on-2 basketball.** The over-the-top arcade style is defined by two-on-two, turbo, legal\n71\tcontact, impossible dunks, a hot-streak power and loud personality. Those live best in short matches; a four-game story\n72\tladder (the Tour) gives the demo an arc and story beats; Exhibition, local multiplayer and online show the rest.\n73\t\n74\t**Perspective: a side-on broadcast camera.** `const BROADCAST = new E.View('broadcast', 'Broadcast', 0, 30, 1, 1)`: yaw\n75\t0, pitch 30°, one screen pixel per world unit. The court's length runs left to right (world x), the camera sits beyond\n76\tthe near sideline looking across it, and pans horizontally with the ball. Why: the race up and down the court reads\n77\tleft/right, dunks and blocks read in height at a low pitch (z is drawn at cos 30° = 0.87, the court's depth at\n78\tsin 30° = 0.5), and below pitch 40 the engine draws rigs from the view itself, so jump poses read. The engine's\n79\t`'threequarter'` view (pitch 55) is offered as Settings → Camera → High; every custom draw uses `r.w()` projection so\n80\tboth views work (yaw is 0 in both, so the depth rules in §9 hold in both).\n81\t\n82\t**Look: the engine's native HD style, pushed to \"90s arcade broadcast\".** `res: 'ps1'` with `expand: true`; HD\n83\tHumanoids at size 1.6–1.95 (players 48–56 world units tall, about 42–48 px on screen); textured court floors painted by\n84\t`floorTex`; an `E.Backdrop` plus hand-drawn silhouettes behind every court; 3–8 props per screen; canvas lighting on\n85\tevery court; `E.font.title` logos and comic banners (\"BLAM!\", \"REJECTED!\"); particles, hit-stop, shake and flashes on\n86\tevery impact; broadcast graphics (a score bug, lower-third captions with live portraits). Saturated team colors, neon\n87\tat the night courts, chunky outlines. Compare screenshots with PS1-era 2D, not NES.\n88\t\n89\t---\n90\t\n91\t## 3. Tech stack and setup\n92\t\n93\t- **Engine:** my-3D2dge **v0.85.2** (planned against repo commit `4390a985`, 2026-11-03), the full readable engine from\n94\t  `dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js` (it already contains every module, including `arcade`). We change it where the game needs\n95\t  (§5). If the repository has moved on, read the newer handoff `README.md` and `CHANGELOG.md` head for breaking changes.\n96\t- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client **1.4.0** (`lib/hub/session.js`, ai-hub commit `f8835e48`), pasted\n97\t  unmodified as the first script in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`.\n98\t- **Code:** plain JavaScript in one IIFE (`const E = My3D2dge` first), no frameworks, no runtime network requests, no\n99\t  CDN at runtime (the hub forbids network traffic outside the hub; the bundle must be self-contained).\n100\t- **Tests:** Node 24 (`node:vm` headless runs), Playwright 1.63.0 with Chromium (the handoff's pin) for browser checks,\n101\t  `python3 -m http.server` for the hub harness.\n102\t\n103\t**Setup (from the root folder):**\n104\t\n105\t```sh\n106\tDEV=Basketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174822\n107\tmkdir -p $DEV && cd $DEV\n108\tgit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git .engine-src\n109\tgit -C .engine-src sparse-checkout set dist/handoff && cp -r .engine-src/dist/handoff handoff && rm -rf .engine-src\n110\tmkdir -p engine vendor/hub-harness && cp handoff/my-3d2dge.js engine/my-3d2dge.js\n111\tB=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub\n112\tcurl -fsSL $B/session.js -o vendor/aihub-session.js\n113\tfor f in harness.html bridge.js round.js README.md; do curl -fsSL $B/$f -o vendor/hub-harness/$f; done\n114\tnpm init -y >/dev/null && npm pkg set type=module && npm install -D playwright@1.63.0\n115\tnpx playwright install chromium     # skip if ~/.cache/ms-playwright already has a matching chromium\n116\t```\n117\t\n118\t**Read before coding** (in `handoff/`): `README.md`, `API.md` (whole), `kits/sports.js` (whole: our starting point for\n119\tthe ball, passes, `E.arc` lobs, formations, `allowJoin`, the proof and the bot), `game/test.mjs` and `kits/test.mjs`\n120\t(headless testing), `docs/ASSETS.md` (whole, before writing any shape), and grep `API-INDEX.md` for names.\n121\t`vendor/hub-harness/README.md` is the hub protocol: read it whole before §15's milestone.\n122\t\n123\t**Environment rules** (from TASK.md, made concrete):\n124\t- Read only what you need; other agents share the parent directory, the machine's ports and its processes.\n125\t- Prefer `file://` pages and the engine's headless tools. The hub harness needs a server: bind\n126\t  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` (the OS picks the port; parse it from the server's first line), fetch\n127\t  `/game.html` and check its `<title>` is Basketblam's before trusting a result, and stop only that server, by its PID.\n128\t  Never `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`, or kill whatever holds a port.\n129\t- /tmp was 99% full on this machine while this plan was written. If npm, git or Playwright fail with `ENOSPC`, export\n130\t  `TMPDIR=$PWD/.tmp` (inside the dev folder) and delete that folder when done.\n131\t- Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit (TASK.md).\n132\t\n133\t---\n134\t\n135\t## 4. Architecture\n136\t\n137\t### 4.1 Shape of the program\n138\t\n139\tOne `E.Game`, a set of scenes, and game systems that are plain objects working on one match state. The match\n140\tsimulation never reads devices directly: every player slot reads a **command** each step from an **input source**\n141\t(local device, CPU brain, network, attract director). That one rule gives local multiplayer, AI teammates, the attract\n142\tmode, the dev mode's autopilot, the test bots and online play for the same code.\n143\t\n144\t```js\n145\tconst HUB = !!(window.AIHub && AIHub.active);               // true from the first line inside a hub session\n146\tconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: BROADCAST, views: ['broadcast', 'threequarter'],\n147\t  bg: '#05040a', id: 'basketblam', input: KEYS, autoPause: !HUB,\n148\t  menu: HUB ? false : { items: pauseItems, settings: GAME_SETTINGS, controls: CONTROL_NAMES, quit: 'title' } });\n149\t```\n150\t\n151\t`pauseItems` is a function returning the list for the current context (the Shell evaluates `menu.items` when the pause\n152\tscreen opens and skips falsy entries; confirm in `Shell` around \"`name === 'pause'`\" and patch the engine if it does\n153\tnot). Items: `How to Play`, `Restart Game` (matches only), `Skip Cutscene` (story only), `Dev Mode`.\n154\t\n155\t### 4.2 Modules (src fragments, concatenated in this order into one IIFE)\n156\t\n157\t| File | Holds |\n158\t|---|---|\n159\t| `00-core.js` | `E` alias, constants (§6.1), `HUB` flag, the `game`, input preset `KEYS`, settings specs, the shared state `S`, the event bus, RNG streams, registries (`def`, `get`, `all`) |\n160\t| `01-tuning.js` | `TUNING` defaults, live values and slider metadata (Appendix A); `tune(key)` reads; `resetTuning()` |\n161\t| `02-data-teams.js` | teams, players (ratings, rig specs, voices, taunts, celebrations), NPCs |\n162\t| `03-data-courts.js` | courts (palette, floor style, backdrop, props, crowd, lights, music, twist), arena layout |\n163\t| `04-data-story.js` | the Tour, story beats (scripts), matchup trash talk, halftime gags, sponsor cards, credits and About text |\n164\t| `05-data-lines.js` | announcer line banks by event |\n165\t| `06-data-moves.js` | animation clips, dunk catalog, taunts, power-ups, cheat codes |\n166\t| `10-audio-music.js` | songs, stings, dynamic layers, crowd ambience |\n167\t| `11-audio-sfx.js` | sound definitions (`audio.define`), material-aware dribble, sound policy |\n168\t| `12-audio-voice.js` | the gibberish voice synth (§12.3) and its Dialog/caption hookup |\n169\t| `20-court-build.js` | arena TileMap, floor painting (lines, logos), stands, collision bounds, shapes for props |\n170\t| `21-court-draw.js` | hoops (stanchion, cheated backboard, rim halves, net simulation, shatter), crowd, backdrop silhouettes, lights |\n171\t| `22-twists.js` | Jackpot Spot, wind, oil slicks, eruptions and steam vents, Dome pyro, boss mechanics |\n172\t| `30-ball.js` | ball states, flight, floor/rim/backboard collisions, scoring detection, net contact, trails |\n173\t| `31-player.js` | player entity: body, ratings, state machine, turbo, juice, power-up effects |\n174\t| `32-anim.js` | rig factory (dressing), pose layer over `solvePose`, clips, dribble sync, dunk choreography |\n175\t| `33-actions.js` | shot model, passes and alley-oops, steals, shoves, spin moves, blocks, goaltending, rebounds |\n176\t| `34-ai.js` | CPU brains (offense, defense, rebounding, teammate commands), difficulty, comeback |\n177\t| `35-match.js` | match flow (tip-off, quarters, clocks, possession, inbounds, overtime), Supernova, shatter pause, power-up spawner, stats, style points |\n178\t| `36-fx.js` | camera director, dunk cam, banners, particle recipes, rumble, comfort-setting policy |\n179\t| `40-ui-kit.js` | 10-foot widgets (focus, cards, buttons, sliders, toggles, pips), glyph icons, prompt lines, mouse hover/click |\n180\t| `41-hud.js` | score bug, player plates, indicators, captions, warnings |\n181\t| `42-screens.js` | title, main menu, join, team/lineup/court select, matchup card, quarter breaks, results, Hall of Blam, How to Play, About/Credits, touch-only notice |\n182\t| `43-tour.js` | tour map, cutscene runner, tour progress and saves, ending, unlocks |\n183\t| `44-camp.js` | Blam Camp lessons |\n184\t| `45-attract.js` | idle detection and the attract director |\n185\t| `46-dev.js` | dev panel, overlays, scene jumps, cheats |\n186\t| `50-hub.js` | AI Hub integration (card, host, client, server, leave, time-up, end) |\n187\t| `90-start.js` | scene table, boot steps, deep links, `game.debug`, `window.__bb`, `game.start` |\n188\t\n189\tRules: content never edits systems (systems read registries); systems talk through the event bus; anything a test or\n190\tthe dev panel needs is reachable from `window.__bb`.\n191\t\n192\t### 4.3 One step (1/120 s) of a match\n193\t\n194\t1. **Commands:** for each of the 4 player slots, its input source writes a `Cmd` (below).\n195\t2. **Players:** state machines consume commands; locomotion; actions start (shots, passes, steals, shoves, spins,\n196\t   jumps); bodies move (`E.Body.update` against the arena map); rigs update (§7).\n197\t3. **Ball:** attached (held/dribbled) or simulated (substeps near rims), collisions, catches, scoring.\n198\t4. **Match:** clocks, possession, dead balls, inbounds, quarter ends, Supernova bookkeeping, spawner, twists.\n199\t5. **Events** emitted during 1–4 are delivered (announcer, audio, crowd, stats, fx, attract director, net).\n200\t6. **Camera director** sets focus, zoom and time scale.\n201\t\n202\t`Cmd` = `{ move: [x, y], shoot: { down, pressed, released }, pass: {...}, turbo: { down }, shove: {...}, oop: {...},\n203\ttaunt: { pressed } }`. Sources: `LocalSource(input)` (from `game.players[i]`), `CpuSource(brain)`, `NetSource(seat)`\n204\t(§15), `ScriptSource(steps)` (tests, attract). Swapping a slot's source is one assignment (dev autopilot, a player\n205\tleaving a hub round, a player joining Exhibition).\n206\t\n207\t### 4.4 State\n208\t\n209\t`S` holds: `scene`, `mode` (`'exhibition' | 'tour' | 'camp' | 'attract' | 'hub'`), `court`, `teams[2]` (team id,\n210\tlineup, bench), `players[4]` (slot 0–1 HOME, 2–3 AWAY), `ball`, `match` (phase, quarter, clock, shotClock, score,\n211\tpossession, alternating arrow, streaks, stats, styleScore), `twist` state, `powerups`, `cheats`, `rng`, `fxRng`,\n212\t`events` (ring buffer of the last 64 for debug and the net), `settings` mirror, `progress` (tour, unlocks, records).\n213\t`serializeMatch()` and `applySnapshot()` (§15) live beside it from the first milestone, with a test.\n214\t\n215\t### 4.5 Events\n216\t\n217\t`emit(type, data)` / `on(type, fn)`. Types: `tipoff, possession, inbound, dribble, pass, catch, intercept, shotStart,\n218\trelease, rim, backboard, basket, miss, airball, block, goaltend, steal, fumble, shove, knockdown, spin, anklesBroken,\n219\trebound, putback, dunkStart, dunk, posterized, alleyoop, supernovaOn, supernovaOff, shatter, sponsorBreak, powerupSpawn,\n220\tpowerupPick, powerupEnd, jackpot, twist (with kind), shotClockViolation, quarterEnd, halftime, overtime, gameEnd,\n221\ttaunt, cheat`. Each carries ids, positions and context (`points`, `dunkId`, `dist`, `contested`, `perfect`...).\n222\t\n223\t### 4.6 Randomness and determinism\n224\t\n225\t- `S.rng = E.rng(seed)`: every gameplay decision (shot outcomes, AI choices, steals, spawns, twist timing).\n226\t- `S.fxRng`: cosmetic choices (crowd, which line the announcer says). Particles may use `Math.random`.\n227\t- Tests seed `Math.random` too (as the kits' `proof()` does) so the rigs and particles repeat.\n228\t- Same seed + same commands = same match. A `proof(n)` function hashes the state after n scripted steps (copy the\n229\t  shell's `proof()` from `kits/sports.js`).\n230\t\n231\t### 4.7 Tuning\n232\t\n233\t`TUNING` is the single home of the important numbers (Appendix A): `{ key: { v, def, min, max, step, group, label,\n234\thelp } }`. Systems read `tune('runSpeed')` every time (never cache), so a dev slider changes play at once.\n235\t`resetTuning()` restores `def`. Dev mode can save the current values to `E.store('bb:tuning')` and copy them as JSON.\n236\t\n237\t### 4.8 Persistence\n238\t\n239\t`E.store` keys: `bb:progress` (`{ tour: { team, stop, score, cheatsUsed, beatsSeen }, unlocked: { bots, allstars,\n240\tbigHeads }, camp: [done lesson ids], records: { mostPoints, bestDunk, toursWon } }`), `bb:tuning`. The Tour also uses\n241\t`game.saves` autosave (`write`/`read`, `autosave()` after each stop, `latest()` for CONTINUE TOUR). Hall of Blam\n242\ttables use `E.Arcade` (`id: 'basketblam'`, mode `'tour'`). In hub sessions the client gives the game an empty\n243\tin-memory storage: nothing to do.\n244\t\n245\t### 4.9 Deep links and the debug handle\n246\t\n247\tDeep links (the page's `#hash`, read in `90-start.js`): `#title`, `#attract`, `#exhibition/<court>/<home>-<away>`,\n248\t`#tour/<stop 1-4>`, `#story/<beatId>`, `#camp/<lessonId>`, `#results`, `#credits`, `#about`, `#dev` (an exhibition with\n249\tthe dev panel open), `#animlab`, `#shootaround`. `#animlab` (every clip on a rig, slow motion, scrub) and\n250\t`#shootaround` (rim physics bench) are dev tools, also reachable from the dev panel's SCENES tab.\n251\t\n252\t`game.debug = { snapshot(), teleport(slot, x, y), give(slot), setScore(a, b), setClock(s), setQuarter(q), force(name,\n253\targs), go(scene, data), spawnPowerup(id, x, y), cheat(name, on), tune(key, v) }`. `snapshot()` returns `{ scene, mode,\n254\tcourt, phase, quarter, clock, shotClock, score, possession, ball: { x, y, z, state, owner }, players: [{ slot, id, team,\n255\tx, y, z, state, turbo, juice, nova, powerup, source }], lastEvents: [10 types], ui: { screen, focus }, attract:\n256\t{ segment }, net: { role, seat, rtt } }`. `window.__bb = { S, game, TUNING, emit, on, debug: game.debug }`.\n257\t\n258\t---\n259\t\n260\t## 5. Engine changes (planned)\n261\t\n262\tMake each in `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, mark it `/* BASKETBLAM: why */`, and list it on the About screen and in the exit\n263\tinterview's engine feedback.\n264\t\n265\t1. **Basketball shorts on `Humanoid`:** option `shorts: { len: .55, sock: '#ffffff', sockLen: .35 }`. In the HD leg\n266\t   drawing (`limb(hip, knee, ..., C.pants)`, `limb(knee, ank, ...)`, about line 4388) draw the thigh in `C.pants` only\n267\t   down to `len` of its length (shorts with a hem in `C.trim`), the rest of the thigh and the shin in `C.skin`, and the\n268\t   last `sockLen` of the shin in `sock`; the same in the classic path (about line 4576).\n269\t2. **Hair styles** `'afro'` (a big shaded ball around the back and top of the head), `'flattop'` (a tall flat box),\n270\t   `'mohawk'` (a ridge of spikes front to back) in the HD head drawing beside `'spiky'` (about line 4493), and in\n271\t   `drawPortrait`.\n272\t3. **`Dialog.voice` may be a function** `(letter, index, pageText)` called for each letter typed (about line 8956),\n273\t   so the gibberish voice speaks the line as it types.\n274\t4. **Public audio buses:** `audio.sfxBus` and `audio.musicBus` getters for `_sfxBus` / `_musBus`, so the voice and crowd\n275\t   nodes follow the volume settings.\n276\t5. Any engine bug found while building: fix it in the engine (not around it), with a test, and log it for the\n277\t   exit interview.\n278\t\n279\tBig heads need no engine change: `rig.o.headR` is read when drawing, so the cheat multiplies it (keep the original to\n280\trestore it).\n281\t\n282\t---\n283\t\n284\t## 6. Court, rules and core mechanics\n285\t\n286\t### 6.1 Court geometry (world units; one tile = 16; z up)\n287\t\n288\tArena map: 48 × 28 tiles (768 × 448). Court-local coordinates `(cx, cy)`: x 0..640 baseline to baseline, y 0..288 far\n289\tsideline to near sideline. World = court-local + `(64, 112)`. Constants (court-local):\n290\t\n291\t| Thing | Value |\n292\t|---|---|\n293\t| Court | 640 × 288; centre (320, 144) |\n294\t| Rim centres | (52, 144) and (588, 144); z 80; inner radius 9; tube radius 1.2 |\n295\t| Backboards (collision) | planes x = 40 and x = 600, y 120..168, z 72..100 |\n296\t| Ball | radius 4.5 |\n297\t| Three-point line | arc radius 136 from the rim centre; straight corner lines at y = 20 and y = 268 from the baseline to x = 108 (mirrored at the other end) |\n298\t| Key (paint) | y 96..192, baseline to x = 152 (free-throw line); free-throw circle r 48 |\n299\t| Centre circle | r 40; half-court line x = 320 |\n300\t| Out of bounds | none: invisible walls 24 units outside the lines stop players; the ball bounces off them (the crowd) |\n301\t| Players | 48–56 tall; body radius 6; standing reach about z 59 (size 1.7) |\n302\t\n303\tNorth of the court (screen top): an apron (16) and stands of 4 tiers (heights 12/24/36/48) with the crowd. West and\n304\teast: 64 of apron with the stanchion bases, benches, props. South (camera side): 48 of apron with low props (scorer's\n305\ttable, cameras) drawn with `cut` so they never hide play. One player is controlled by each human; there is no switching\n306\tof players (the genre's rule).\n307\t\n308\t### 6.2 Controls and actions\n309\t\n310\t| Action | Pad (standard mapping) | Keyboard | Mouse | With the ball | Without the ball |\n311\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n312\t| move | left stick, d-pad | WASD (arrows for a lone player too) | – | run / dribble | run, defend |\n313\t| `shoot` | □ Square (`Pad2`) | Space, J | left button | hold: jump; release at the top to shoot; in dunk range with turbo: dunk | jump to block or rebound; if your CPU teammate has the ball: \"shoot it!\" |\n314\t| `pass` | ✕ Cross (`Pad0`) | E, K | right button | pass to your teammate (led) | steal swipe; if your CPU teammate has the ball: \"pass it to me!\" |\n315\t| `turbo` | R2 (`Pad7`), R1 (`Pad5`) | Shift (left or right) | – | hold: faster, higher, dunks | hold: faster, higher |\n316\t| `shove` | ○ Circle (`Pad1`) | Q, L | – | SPIN MOVE | SHOVE |\n317\t| `oop` | △ Triangle (`Pad3`) | F, I | – | alley-oop lob to your teammate | call for the ball; CPU teammate with the ball throws you an oop if you're in range |\n318\t| `taunt` | L1 (`Pad4`) | R, U | – | – | taunt (dead balls or without the ball; off in hub sessions) |\n319\t| `pause` / menus | Options, ✕/○ | Esc/P, Enter/Esc | click, right-click back | | |\n320\t\n321\tPreset: `const KEYS = Object.assign({}, E.Input.DEFAULT, { shoot: ['Space', 'KeyJ', 'Mouse0', 'Pad2'], pass: ['KeyE',\n322\t'KeyK', 'Mouse2', 'Pad0'], turbo: ['ShiftLeft', 'ShiftRight', 'Pad7', 'Pad5'], shove: ['KeyQ', 'KeyL', 'Pad1'], oop:\n323\t['KeyF', 'KeyI', 'Pad3'], taunt: ['KeyR', 'KeyU', 'Pad4'] })`, minus `attack, dash, skill, jump`. Names for the Controls\n324\tscreen: `{ shoot: 'Shoot / Block', pass: 'Pass / Steal', turbo: 'Turbo', shove: 'Shove / Spin', oop: 'Alley-oop / Call',\n325\ttaunt: 'Taunt' }`. Two players on one keyboard get the engine's halves (`E.Input.halfKeys`): check what it assigns and\n326\tshow it on the join screen. Combo shortcut kept for genre veterans: turbo + pass without the ball also shoves.\n327\t\n328\t### 6.3 Match flow and rules (\"What you can and can't do\")\n329\t\n330\t- **2 on 2, full court, 4 quarters.** Quarter length: Exhibition from Settings (1:00 / 1:30 / 2:00 / 3:00, default\n331\t  2:00); Tour 1:30; hub 2 halves of 1:30. The game clock runs only while the ball is live; it stops on made baskets\n332\t  until the inbound is caught, and during breaks.\n333\t- **Tip-off** starts the game and each overtime: the two jumpers (highest SWAT on each side) at centre; REF ROSCOE\n334\t  tosses the ball (apex z 120 at 0.9 s); press shoot to jump; the jump whose hands peak nearest the ball's apex wins\n335\t  (CPU timing error by difficulty) and taps it to the teammate. Quarters 2–4 begin with an inbound at half court by the\n336\t  alternating arrow (the team that lost the tip gets Q2 and Q3).\n337\t- **Scoring:** 2 points; 3 from beyond the arc (by the shooter's takeoff point); dunks 2; Jackpot Spot +2; Gold Ball\n338\t  doubles. A shot released before the buzzer counts.\n339\t- **No fouls.** Shoving, steals and body contact are legal. No traveling, no backcourt, no three seconds, no out of\n340\t  bounds (the walls keep everything in).\n341\t- **Shot clock 20 s;** resets on a change of possession and when a shot touches the rim. Expiry: buzzer, turnover,\n342\t  inbound by the other team from the nearest sideline.\n343\t- **Goaltending:** touching the ball while it is coming down (vz < 0) within 18 units of the rim centre and above the\n344\t  rim counts the basket for the shooter (\"GOALTEND!\"). Supernova players and BLAMBOTS ignore this rule.\n345\t- **After a made basket** the other team inbounds from under the basket it defends: the inbounder passes in after 0.8 s\n346\t  (or when its human presses pass). Defenders may not stand within 24 units of the inbounder; the inbound pass can be\n347\t  intercepted only by a defender standing in its lane.\n348\t- **Tie after Q4:** overtime of 1:00, again if tied; after two overtimes, SUDDEN BLAM: the next basket wins (so every\n349\t  game ends, which the tests rely on).\n350\t- **Breaks:** after Q1 and Q3 a short break card (6 s, subs allowed); after Q2 the Halftime Report (stats, a gag, subs).\n351\t\n352\t### 6.4 Ratings\n353\t\n354\tEach player has eight ratings, 1–10, shown as pips with icons (never raw numbers on the 10-foot screens):\n355\t\n356\t| Rating | Means | Effect (r = rating) |\n357\t|---|---|---|\n358\t| ZOOM | speed | run speed × (0.86 + 0.028 r) |\n359\t| SPLASH | two-point jumpers, layups | make chance × (0.78 + 0.045 r) |\n360\t| DEEP | three-pointers | make chance × (0.70 + 0.06 r) |\n361\t| SLAM | dunking | dunk range × (0.75 + 0.05 r); unlocks dunks (§10.3); resists blocks |\n362\t| DIME | passing, handles | pass speed 180 + 12 r; interception window; ball security vs steals; alley-oop range |\n363\t| SWIPE | steals | steal chance × (0.55 + 0.09 r); steal reach 14 + 0.6 r |\n364\t| SWAT | blocks, rebounds | jump speed 250 + 10 r; block reach 10 + 0.8 r; block chance |\n365\t| BUMP | power | shove force 140 + 14 r; knockdown time; fumble chance; shove resistance; box-outs |\n366\t\n367\tEvery effect is multiplied by the juice factor (§6.14) and by power-ups and Supernova where they apply.\n368\t\n369\t### 6.5 Movement and turbo\n370\t\n371\t- Velocity approaches `stick × runSpeed × ZOOM factor` at `accel` (900 u/s²). Facing turns toward the movement; on\n372\t  defense with the opponent holding the ball, the defender faces the ball handler and shuffles (defensive stance).\n373\t- Bodies are `E.Body` (radius 6, gravity `playerGravity` 900) on the arena TileMap; players push each other apart\n374\t  (separation, no damage); `E.SpatialHash` is unnecessary at four players.\n375\t- **Turbo** (0..1 per player): held and moving drains `turboDrain` 0.30/s; not held refills `turboRefill` 0.20/s after\n376\t  `turboRefillDelay` 0.35 s; speed × `turboMult` 1.5, jumps × 1.15; required to start a dunk (≥ 0.05); a shove costs\n377\t  0.25, a spin move 0.2. Supernova and Rocket Kicks change it (§6.12, §6.15).\n378\t- A sharp change of direction at speed squeaks the sneakers and kicks dust.\n379\t\n380\t### 6.6 The ball\n381\t\n382\tStates: `held` (by its owner's hands), `dribble` (owner; position from the dribble cycle, §7), `pass` (flying to a\n383\ttarget), `shot` (flying with its shot record: shooter, points, decided outcome, release time, perfect flag), `loose`\n384\t(free physics), `dead` (scripted: after a score, during breaks), `inbound`.\n385\t\n386\t- Gravity `ballGravity` 760; floor bounce restitution `ballBounce` 0.62 with friction; rolling decays.\n387\t- **Rim:** a torus (ring radius 9, tube 1.2). Closest point Q on the ring circle to the ball's centre; if\n388\t  `|ball − Q| < 4.5 + 1.2` reflect the velocity along the normal with `rimBounce` 0.45 and a little friction; the rim\n389\t  \"rattles\" (visual bend + clank). Substep the ball 4× (1/480 s) within 40 units of a rim; never let it tunnel.\n390\t- **Backboard:** the plane x = 40 (or 600) within its y/z bounds; restitution 0.55; a thud.\n391\t- **Arena walls:** 24 outside the lines; restitution 0.5.\n392\t- **Basket:** the ball's centre crosses z = 80 downward within 6.75 of the rim centre: score once (a `scored` flag\n393\t  holds until it falls below z 60); the net slows it (vz × 0.6) as it passes.\n394\t- Players do not collide with the ball except to catch, steal, block, tip or goaltend it.\n395\t\n396\t### 6.7 Shooting\n397\t\n398\tPressing shoot with the ball (out of dunk range, or without turbo) starts a **jump shot**: gather 0.08 s, jump\n399\t(`jumpShotVz` 250 + SWAT bonus), the ball rises above the forehead. Releasing the button releases the ball; holding past\n400\tthe top releases it automatically 0.25 s after the top (a late release). The **release meter** (a thin arc around the"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"213edd46-9651-46f8-a282-da1d89430eab","uuid":"2b95477b-a724-453f-8cd9-01e213a37333","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:16:23.269Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-build--20261104-061612--x5a4f79-work/213edd46-9651-46f8-a282-da1d89430eab/tool-results/blpovl870.txt","content":"# BASKETBLAM\n\n**Over-the-top 2-on-2 arcade basketball. No fouls. No mercy. All BLAM.**\n\nThis is the build plan for the demo TASK.md describes. It is written for the developers (people or AI models) who will\nbuild the game from TASK.md and this file alone, without the planner. Read TASK.md first, then this file once, top to\nbottom; after that use it as a reference. Everything here is a decision, not a menu: where a number is given, start\nwith it and tune it with the dev sliders (§14). Where this file and TASK.md disagree, TASK.md wins.\n\n---\n\n## 0. At a glance\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Title | **BASKETBLAM** (on screen); `Basketblam` in file names |\n| Genre | Arcade sports: 2-on-2 basketball in the over-the-top arcade style, with a short story ladder |\n| Perspective | Side-on **broadcast camera**: a custom orthographic view, pitch 30°, the court running left to right, panning with the ball; a dunk cam that zooms; a \"High\" camera option |\n| Players | 1–4 on one machine (gamepads and a shared keyboard); 1–4 online in AI Hub sessions (shared world) |\n| Modes | Blam Tour (story, 4 games), Exhibition (any teams and courts, 1–4 players), Blam Camp (interactive tutorial), Hall of Blam (records), How to Play, Options, About/Credits; attract mode; dev mode; AI Hub \"Showdown\" |\n| Content | 5 courts, each with its own twist; 6 teams (4 street crews, a robot boss team, a secret team), 18 players and 5 story characters; 12 dunks; 6 power-ups; 10 tutorial lessons; 15 story beats; ~200 announcer lines; 12 songs and 8 stings; about 55 sound effects; 21 voice profiles |\n| Tech | my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, our modified copy), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript, Canvas 2D, Web Audio; Node 24 + Playwright 1.63 for tests |\n| Deliverable | one self-contained HTML file in the root, plus `exit-interview.json` beside it |\n\n**The core loop, in one sentence:** get the ball, beat your defender with turbo, spin moves and passes, and finish with a\ndunk that leaves the atmosphere or a jumper released at the top of the jump; on defense swipe, swat and shove (it's\nlegal); score three straight and you go **SUPERNOVA**.\n\n**Pillars** (use them to settle any question this plan doesn't):\n1. **Every possession can end in spectacle.** Dunks, blocks, shoves and steals are big, readable and loud.\n2. **Simple buttons, real timing.** Four main buttons; skill lives in release timing, turbo management, alley-oop\n   timing and steal angles.\n3. **Personality everywhere.** Two hype announcers, trash talk, courts with twists, a crowd that reacts.\n4. **Couch first, 10 feet away.** Up to four on one screen, a TV-readable HUD, controller-first menus.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Title, names and folders\n\n- **Title: BASKETBLAM.** Picked as TASK.md asks: fifty riffs on \"basketball\" were brainstormed and one was chosen with a\n  real random number generator (Python `secrets.randbelow(50)` returned 18). Do not re-pick it.\n- **SOURCE token** in file names: `Basketball`.\n- **Names for this TASK.md** (copy them exactly):\n  - dev folder: `Basketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174822/` (in the root, beside TASK.md)\n  - deliverable: `Basketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174822.html` (in the root)\n  - exit interview: `exit-interview.json` (in the root, beside the HTML)\n\n  If your TASK.md names another model or timestamp, use your TASK.md's names with the same `Basketblam` and\n  `Basketball` tokens.\n- **Dev folder layout:**\n\n```\nBasketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174822/\n  handoff/            pristine copy of my-3d2dge dist/handoff (docs, tools/check.mjs, filmstrip.mjs, asset tools)\n  engine/my-3d2dge.js our modified engine (every change marked /* BASKETBLAM: ... */)\n  vendor/aihub-session.js       AI Hub client 1.4.0, byte for byte\n  vendor/hub-harness/           harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (tests only, never bundled)\n  src/*.js            the game, as numbered fragments (§4.2)\n  tools/build.mjs     concatenates src into build/game.js, writes build/index.html and the root deliverable\n  tests/run.mjs       the one test command (§19); tests/*.test.mjs; tests/helpers.mjs; tests/fakehub.mjs\n  build/              generated: game.js, index.html (dev page), hub/ (served for harness tests)\n  check-output/       screenshots and filmstrips for review (generated)\n  package.json        { \"type\": \"module\", devDependencies: { \"playwright\": \"1.63.0\" } }\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Genre, perspective and look\n\n**Genre: arcade sports, 2-on-2 basketball.** The over-the-top arcade style is defined by two-on-two, turbo, legal\ncontact, impossible dunks, a hot-streak power and loud personality. Those live best in short matches; a four-game story\nladder (the Tour) gives the demo an arc and story beats; Exhibition, local multiplayer and online show the rest.\n\n**Perspective: a side-on broadcast camera.** `const BROADCAST = new E.View('broadcast', 'Broadcast', 0, 30, 1, 1)`: yaw\n0, pitch 30°, one screen pixel per world unit. The court's length runs left to right (world x), the camera sits beyond\nthe near sideline looking across it, and pans horizontally with the ball. Why: the race up and down the court reads\nleft/right, dunks and blocks read in height at a low pitch (z is drawn at cos 30° = 0.87, the court's depth at\nsin 30° = 0.5), and below pitch 40 the engine draws rigs from the view itself, so jump poses read. The engine's\n`'threequarter'` view (pitch 55) is offered as Settings → Camera → High; every custom draw uses `r.w()` projection so\nboth views work (yaw is 0 in both, so the depth rules in §9 hold in both).\n\n**Look: the engine's native HD style, pushed to \"90s arcade broadcast\".** `res: 'ps1'` with `expand: true`; HD\nHumanoids at size 1.6–1.95 (players 48–56 world units tall, about 42–48 px on screen); textured court floors painted by\n`floorTex`; an `E.Backdrop` plus hand-drawn silhouettes behind every court; 3–8 props per screen; canvas lighting on\nevery court; `E.font.title` logos and comic banners (\"BLAM!\", \"REJECTED!\"); particles, hit-stop, shake and flashes on\nevery impact; broadcast graphics (a score bug, lower-third captions with live portraits). Saturated team colors, neon\nat the night courts, chunky outlines. Compare screenshots with PS1-era 2D, not NES.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Tech stack and setup\n\n- **Engine:** my-3D2dge **v0.85.2** (planned against repo commit `4390a985`, 2026-11-03), the full readable engine from\n  `dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js` (it already contains every module, including `arcade`). We change it where the game needs\n  (§5). If the repository has moved on, read the newer handoff `README.md` and `CHANGELOG.md` head for breaking changes.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client **1.4.0** (`lib/hub/session.js`, ai-hub commit `f8835e48`), pasted\n  unmodified as the first script in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`.\n- **Code:** plain JavaScript in one IIFE (`const E = My3D2dge` first), no frameworks, no runtime network requests, no\n  CDN at runtime (the hub forbids network traffic outside the hub; the bundle must be self-contained).\n- **Tests:** Node 24 (`node:vm` headless runs), Playwright 1.63.0 with Chromium (the handoff's pin) for browser checks,\n  `python3 -m http.server` for the hub harness.\n\n**Setup (from the root folder):**\n\n```sh\nDEV=Basketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174822\nmkdir -p $DEV && cd $DEV\ngit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git .engine-src\ngit -C .engine-src sparse-checkout set dist/handoff && cp -r .engine-src/dist/handoff handoff && rm -rf .engine-src\nmkdir -p engine vendor/hub-harness && cp handoff/my-3d2dge.js engine/my-3d2dge.js\nB=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub\ncurl -fsSL $B/session.js -o vendor/aihub-session.js\nfor f in harness.html bridge.js round.js README.md; do curl -fsSL $B/$f -o vendor/hub-harness/$f; done\nnpm init -y >/dev/null && npm pkg set type=module && npm install -D playwright@1.63.0\nnpx playwright install chromium     # skip if ~/.cache/ms-playwright already has a matching chromium\n```\n\n**Read before coding** (in `handoff/`): `README.md`, `API.md` (whole), `kits/sports.js` (whole: our starting point for\nthe ball, passes, `E.arc` lobs, formations, `allowJoin`, the proof and the bot), `game/test.mjs` and `kits/test.mjs`\n(headless testing), `docs/ASSETS.md` (whole, before writing any shape), and grep `API-INDEX.md` for names.\n`vendor/hub-harness/README.md` is the hub protocol: read it whole before §15's milestone.\n\n**Environment rules** (from TASK.md, made concrete):\n- Read only what you need; other agents share the parent directory, the machine's ports and its processes.\n- Prefer `file://` pages and the engine's headless tools. The hub harness needs a server: bind\n  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` (the OS picks the port; parse it from the server's first line), fetch\n  `/game.html` and check its `<title>` is Basketblam's before trusting a result, and stop only that server, by its PID.\n  Never `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`, or kill whatever holds a port.\n- /tmp was 99% full on this machine while this plan was written. If npm, git or Playwright fail with `ENOSPC`, export\n  `TMPDIR=$PWD/.tmp` (inside the dev folder) and delete that folder when done.\n- Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit (TASK.md).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Architecture\n\n### 4.1 Shape of the program\n\nOne `E.Game`, a set of scenes, and game systems that are plain objects working on one match state. The match\nsimulation never reads devices directly: every player slot reads a **command** each step from an **input source**\n(local device, CPU brain, network, attract director). That one rule gives local multiplayer, AI teammates, the attract\nmode, the dev mode's autopilot, the test bots and online play for the same code.\n\n```js\nconst HUB = !!(window.AIHub && AIHub.active);               // true from the first line inside a hub session\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: BROADCAST, views: ['broadcast', 'threequarter'],\n  bg: '#05040a', id: 'basketblam', input: KEYS, autoPause: !HUB,\n  menu: HUB ? false : { items: pauseItems, settings: GAME_SETTINGS, controls: CONTROL_NAMES, quit: 'title' } });\n```\n\n`pauseItems` is a function returning the list for the current context (the Shell evaluates `menu.items` when the pause\nscreen opens and skips falsy entries; confirm in `Shell` around \"`name === 'pause'`\" and patch the engine if it does\nnot). Items: `How to Play`, `Restart Game` (matches only), `Skip Cutscene` (story only), `Dev Mode`.\n\n### 4.2 Modules (src fragments, concatenated in this order into one IIFE)\n\n| File | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| `00-core.js` | `E` alias, constants (§6.1), `HUB` flag, the `game`, input preset `KEYS`, settings specs, the shared state `S`, the event bus, RNG streams, registries (`def`, `get`, `all`) |\n| `01-tuning.js` | `TUNING` defaults, live values and slider metadata (Appendix A); `tune(key)` reads; `resetTuning()` |\n| `02-data-teams.js` | teams, players (ratings, rig specs, voices, taunts, celebrations), NPCs |\n| `03-data-courts.js` | courts (palette, floor style, backdrop, props, crowd, lights, music, twist), arena layout |\n| `04-data-story.js` | the Tour, story beats (scripts), matchup trash talk, halftime gags, sponsor cards, credits and About text |\n| `05-data-lines.js` | announcer line banks by event |\n| `06-data-moves.js` | animation clips, dunk catalog, taunts, power-ups, cheat codes |\n| `10-audio-music.js` | songs, stings, dynamic layers, crowd ambience |\n| `11-audio-sfx.js` | sound definitions (`audio.define`), material-aware dribble, sound policy |\n| `12-audio-voice.js` | the gibberish voice synth (§12.3) and its Dialog/caption hookup |\n| `20-court-build.js` | arena TileMap, floor painting (lines, logos), stands, collision bounds, shapes for props |\n| `21-court-draw.js` | hoops (stanchion, cheated backboard, rim halves, net simulation, shatter), crowd, backdrop silhouettes, lights |\n| `22-twists.js` | Jackpot Spot, wind, oil slicks, eruptions and steam vents, Dome pyro, boss mechanics |\n| `30-ball.js` | ball states, flight, floor/rim/backboard collisions, scoring detection, net contact, trails |\n| `31-player.js` | player entity: body, ratings, state machine, turbo, juice, power-up effects |\n| `32-anim.js` | rig factory (dressing), pose layer over `solvePose`, clips, dribble sync, dunk choreography |\n| `33-actions.js` | shot model, passes and alley-oops, steals, shoves, spin moves, blocks, goaltending, rebounds |\n| `34-ai.js` | CPU brains (offense, defense, rebounding, teammate commands), difficulty, comeback |\n| `35-match.js` | match flow (tip-off, quarters, clocks, possession, inbounds, overtime), Supernova, shatter pause, power-up spawner, stats, style points |\n| `36-fx.js` | camera director, dunk cam, banners, particle recipes, rumble, comfort-setting policy |\n| `40-ui-kit.js` | 10-foot widgets (focus, cards, buttons, sliders, toggles, pips), glyph icons, prompt lines, mouse hover/click |\n| `41-hud.js` | score bug, player plates, indicators, captions, warnings |\n| `42-screens.js` | title, main menu, join, team/lineup/court select, matchup card, quarter breaks, results, Hall of Blam, How to Play, About/Credits, touch-only notice |\n| `43-tour.js` | tour map, cutscene runner, tour progress and saves, ending, unlocks |\n| `44-camp.js` | Blam Camp lessons |\n| `45-attract.js` | idle detection and the attract director |\n| `46-dev.js` | dev panel, overlays, scene jumps, cheats |\n| `50-hub.js` | AI Hub integration (card, host, client, server, leave, time-up, end) |\n| `90-start.js` | scene table, boot steps, deep links, `game.debug`, `window.__bb`, `game.start` |\n\nRules: content never edits systems (systems read registries); systems talk through the event bus; anything a test or\nthe dev panel needs is reachable from `window.__bb`.\n\n### 4.3 One step (1/120 s) of a match\n\n1. **Commands:** for each of the 4 player slots, its input source writes a `Cmd` (below).\n2. **Players:** state machines consume commands; locomotion; actions start (shots, passes, steals, shoves, spins,\n   jumps); bodies move (`E.Body.update` against the arena map); rigs update (§7).\n3. **Ball:** attached (held/dribbled) or simulated (substeps near rims), collisions, catches, scoring.\n4. **Match:** clocks, possession, dead balls, inbounds, quarter ends, Supernova bookkeeping, spawner, twists.\n5. **Events** emitted during 1–4 are delivered (announcer, audio, crowd, stats, fx, attract director, net).\n6. **Camera director** sets focus, zoom and time scale.\n\n`Cmd` = `{ move: [x, y], shoot: { down, pressed, released }, pass: {...}, turbo: { down }, shove: {...}, oop: {...},\ntaunt: { pressed } }`. Sources: `LocalSource(input)` (from `game.players[i]`), `CpuSource(brain)`, `NetSource(seat)`\n(§15), `ScriptSource(steps)` (tests, attract). Swapping a slot's source is one assignment (dev autopilot, a player\nleaving a hub round, a player joining Exhibition).\n\n### 4.4 State\n\n`S` holds: `scene`, `mode` (`'exhibition' | 'tour' | 'camp' | 'attract' | 'hub'`), `court`, `teams[2]` (team id,\nlineup, bench), `players[4]` (slot 0–1 HOME, 2–3 AWAY), `ball`, `match` (phase, quarter, clock, shotClock, score,\npossession, alternating arrow, streaks, stats, styleScore), `twist` state, `powerups`, `cheats`, `rng`, `fxRng`,\n`events` (ring buffer of the last 64 for debug and the net), `settings` mirror, `progress` (tour, unlocks, records).\n`serializeMatch()` and `applySnapshot()` (§15) live beside it from the first milestone, with a test.\n\n### 4.5 Events\n\n`emit(type, data)` / `on(type, fn)`. Types: `tipoff, possession, inbound, dribble, pass, catch, intercept, shotStart,\nrelease, rim, backboard, basket, miss, airball, block, goaltend, steal, fumble, shove, knockdown, spin, anklesBroken,\nrebound, putback, dunkStart, dunk, posterized, alleyoop, supernovaOn, supernovaOff, shatter, sponsorBreak, powerupSpawn,\npowerupPick, powerupEnd, jackpot, twist (with kind), shotClockViolation, quarterEnd, halftime, overtime, gameEnd,\ntaunt, cheat`. Each carries ids, positions and context (`points`, `dunkId`, `dist`, `contested`, `perfect`...).\n\n### 4.6 Randomness and determinism\n\n- `S.rng = E.rng(seed)`: every gameplay decision (shot outcomes, AI choices, steals, spawns, twist timing).\n- `S.fxRng`: cosmetic choices (crowd, which line the announcer says). Particles may use `Math.random`.\n- Tests seed `Math.random` too (as the kits' `proof()` does) so the rigs and particles repeat.\n- Same seed + same commands = same match. A `proof(n)` function hashes the state after n scripted steps (copy the\n  shell's `proof()` from `kits/sports.js`).\n\n### 4.7 Tuning\n\n`TUNING` is the single home of the important numbers (Appendix A): `{ key: { v, def, min, max, step, group, label,\nhelp } }`. Systems read `tune('runSpeed')` every time (never cache), so a dev slider changes play at once.\n`resetTuning()` restores `def`. Dev mode can save the current values to `E.store('bb:tuning')` and copy them as JSON.\n\n### 4.8 Persistence\n\n`E.store` keys: `bb:progress` (`{ tour: { team, stop, score, cheatsUsed, beatsSeen }, unlocked: { bots, allstars,\nbigHeads }, camp: [done lesson ids], records: { mostPoints, bestDunk, toursWon } }`), `bb:tuning`. The Tour also uses\n`game.saves` autosave (`write`/`read`, `autosave()` after each stop, `latest()` for CONTINUE TOUR). Hall of Blam\ntables use `E.Arcade` (`id: 'basketblam'`, mode `'tour'`). In hub sessions the client gives the game an empty\nin-memory storage: nothing to do.\n\n### 4.9 Deep links and the debug handle\n\nDeep links (the page's `#hash`, read in `90-start.js`): `#title`, `#attract`, `#exhibition/<court>/<home>-<away>`,\n`#tour/<stop 1-4>`, `#story/<beatId>`, `#camp/<lessonId>`, `#results`, `#credits`, `#about`, `#dev` (an exhibition with\nthe dev panel open), `#animlab`, `#shootaround`. `#animlab` (every clip on a rig, slow motion, scrub) and\n`#shootaround` (rim physics bench) are dev tools, also reachable from the dev panel's SCENES tab.\n\n`game.debug = { snapshot(), teleport(slot, x, y), give(slot), setScore(a, b), setClock(s), setQuarter(q), force(name,\nargs), go(scene, data), spawnPowerup(id, x, y), cheat(name, on), tune(key, v) }`. `snapshot()` returns `{ scene, mode,\ncourt, phase, quarter, clock, shotClock, score, possession, ball: { x, y, z, state, owner }, players: [{ slot, id, team,\nx, y, z, state, turbo, juice, nova, powerup, source }], lastEvents: [10 types], ui: { screen, focus }, attract:\n{ segment }, net: { role, seat, rtt } }`. `window.__bb = { S, game, TUNING, emit, on, debug: game.debug }`.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Engine changes (planned)\n\nMake each in `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, mark it `/* BASKETBLAM: why */`, and list it on the About screen and in the exit\ninterview's engine feedback.\n\n1. **Basketball shorts on `Humanoid`:** option `shorts: { len: .55, sock: '#ffffff', sockLen: .35 }`. In the HD leg\n   drawing (`limb(hip, knee, ..., C.pants)`, `limb(knee, ank, ...)`, about line 4388) draw the thigh in `C.pants` only\n   down to `len` of its length (shorts with a hem in `C.trim`), the rest of the thigh and the shin in `C.skin`, and the\n   last `sockLen` of the shin in `sock`; the same in the classic path (about line 4576).\n2. **Hair styles** `'afro'` (a big shaded ball around the back and top of the head), `'flattop'` (a tall flat box),\n   `'mohawk'` (a ridge of spikes front to back) in the HD head drawing beside `'spiky'` (about line 4493), and in\n   `drawPortrait`.\n3. **`Dialog.voice` may be a function** `(letter, index, pageText)` called for each letter typed (about line 8956),\n   so the gibberish voice speaks the line as it types.\n4. **Public audio buses:** `audio.sfxBus` and `audio.musicBus` getters for `_sfxBus` / `_musBus`, so the voice and crowd\n   nodes follow the volume settings.\n5. Any engine bug found while building: fix it in the engine (not around it), with a test, and log it for the\n   exit interview.\n\nBig heads need no engine change: `rig.o.headR` is read when drawing, so the cheat multiplies it (keep the original to\nrestore it).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Court, rules and core mechanics\n\n### 6.1 Court geometry (world units; one tile = 16; z up)\n\nArena map: 48 × 28 tiles (768 × 448). Court-local coordinates `(cx, cy)`: x 0..640 baseline to baseline, y 0..288 far\nsideline to near sideline. World = court-local + `(64, 112)`. Constants (court-local):\n\n| Thing | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Court | 640 × 288; centre (320, 144) |\n| Rim centres | (52, 144) and (588, 144); z 80; inner radius 9; tube radius 1.2 |\n| Backboards (collision) | planes x = 40 and x = 600, y 120..168, z 72..100 |\n| Ball | radius 4.5 |\n| Three-point line | arc radius 136 from the rim centre; straight corner lines at y = 20 and y = 268 from the baseline to x = 108 (mirrored at the other end) |\n| Key (paint) | y 96..192, baseline to x = 152 (free-throw line); free-throw circle r 48 |\n| Centre circle | r 40; half-court line x = 320 |\n| Out of bounds | none: invisible walls 24 units outside the lines stop players; the ball bounces off them (the crowd) |\n| Players | 48–56 tall; body radius 6; standing reach about z 59 (size 1.7) |\n\nNorth of the court (screen top): an apron (16) and stands of 4 tiers (heights 12/24/36/48) with the crowd. West and\neast: 64 of apron with the stanchion bases, benches, props. South (camera side): 48 of apron with low props (scorer's\ntable, cameras) drawn with `cut` so they never hide play. One player is controlled by each human; there is no switching\nof players (the genre's rule).\n\n### 6.2 Controls and actions\n\n| Action | Pad (standard mapping) | Keyboard | Mouse | With the ball | Without the ball |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| move | left stick, d-pad | WASD (arrows for a lone player too) | – | run / dribble | run, defend |\n| `shoot` | □ Square (`Pad2`) | Space, J | left button | hold: jump; release at the top to shoot; in dunk range with turbo: dunk | jump to block or rebound; if your CPU teammate has the ball: \"shoot it!\" |\n| `pass` | ✕ Cross (`Pad0`) | E, K | right button | pass to your teammate (led) | steal swipe; if your CPU teammate has the ball: \"pass it to me!\" |\n| `turbo` | R2 (`Pad7`), R1 (`Pad5`) | Shift (left or right) | – | hold: faster, higher, dunks | hold: faster, higher |\n| `shove` | ○ Circle (`Pad1`) | Q, L | – | SPIN MOVE | SHOVE |\n| `oop` | △ Triangle (`Pad3`) | F, I | – | alley-oop lob to your teammate | call for the ball; CPU teammate with the ball throws you an oop if you're in range |\n| `taunt` | L1 (`Pad4`) | R, U | – | – | taunt (dead balls or without the ball; off in hub sessions) |\n| `pause` / menus | Options, ✕/○ | Esc/P, Enter/Esc | click, right-click back | | |\n\nPreset: `const KEYS = Object.assign({}, E.Input.DEFAULT, { shoot: ['Space', 'KeyJ', 'Mouse0', 'Pad2'], pass: ['KeyE',\n'KeyK', 'Mouse2', 'Pad0'], turbo: ['ShiftLeft', 'ShiftRight', 'Pad7', 'Pad5'], shove: ['KeyQ', 'KeyL', 'Pad1'], oop:\n['KeyF', 'KeyI', 'Pad3'], taunt: ['KeyR', 'KeyU', 'Pad4'] })`, minus `attack, dash, skill, jump`. Names for the Controls\nscreen: `{ shoot: 'Shoot / Block', pass: 'Pass / Steal', turbo: 'Turbo', shove: 'Shove / Spin', oop: 'Alley-oop / Call',\ntaunt: 'Taunt' }`. Two players on one keyboard get the engine's halves (`E.Input.halfKeys`): check what it assigns and\nshow it on the join screen. Combo shortcut kept for genre veterans: turbo + pass without the ball also shoves.\n\n### 6.3 Match flow and rules (\"What you can and can't do\")\n\n- **2 on 2, full court, 4 quarters.** Quarter length: Exhibition from Settings (1:00 / 1:30 / 2:00 / 3:00, default\n  2:00); Tour 1:30; hub 2 halves of 1:30. The game clock runs only while the ball is live; it stops on made baskets\n  until the inbound is caught, and during breaks.\n- **Tip-off** starts the game and each overtime: the two jumpers (highest SWAT on each side) at centre; REF ROSCOE\n  tosses the ball (apex z 120 at 0.9 s); press shoot to jump; the jump whose hands peak nearest the ball's apex wins\n  (CPU timing error by difficulty) and taps it to the teammate. Quarters 2–4 begin with an inbound at half court by the\n  alternating arrow (the team that lost the tip gets Q2 and Q3).\n- **Scoring:** 2 points; 3 from beyond the arc (by the shooter's takeoff point); dunks 2; Jackpot Spot +2; Gold Ball\n  doubles. A shot released before the buzzer counts.\n- **No fouls.** Shoving, steals and body contact are legal. No traveling, no backcourt, no three seconds, no out of\n  bounds (the walls keep everything in).\n- **Shot clock 20 s;** resets on a change of possession and when a shot touches the rim. Expiry: buzzer, turnover,\n  inbound by the other team from the nearest sideline.\n- **Goaltending:** touching the ball while it is coming down (vz < 0) within 18 units of the rim centre and above the\n  rim counts the basket for the shooter (\"GOALTEND!\"). Supernova players and BLAMBOTS ignore this rule.\n- **After a made basket** the other team inbounds from under the basket it defends: the inbounder passes in after 0.8 s\n  (or when its human presses pass). Defenders may not stand within 24 units of the inbounder; the inbound pass can be\n  intercepted only by a defender standing in its lane.\n- **Tie after Q4:** overtime of 1:00, again if tied; after two overtimes, SUDDEN BLAM: the next basket wins (so every\n  game ends, which the tests rely on).\n- **Breaks:** after Q1 and Q3 a short break card (6 s, subs allowed); after Q2 the Halftime Report (stats, a gag, subs).\n\n### 6.4 Ratings\n\nEach player has eight ratings, 1–10, shown as pips with icons (never raw numbers on the 10-foot screens):\n\n| Rating | Means | Effect (r = rating) |\n|---|---|---|\n| ZOOM | speed | run speed × (0.86 + 0.028 r) |\n| SPLASH | two-point jumpers, layups | make chance × (0.78 + 0.045 r) |\n| DEEP | three-pointers | make chance × (0.70 + 0.06 r) |\n| SLAM | dunking | dunk range × (0.75 + 0.05 r); unlocks dunks (§10.3); resists blocks |\n| DIME | passing, handles | pass speed 180 + 12 r; interception window; ball security vs steals; alley-oop range |\n| SWIPE | steals | steal chance × (0.55 + 0.09 r); steal reach 14 + 0.6 r |\n| SWAT | blocks, rebounds | jump speed 250 + 10 r; block reach 10 + 0.8 r; block chance |\n| BUMP | power | shove force 140 + 14 r; knockdown time; fumble chance; shove resistance; box-outs |\n\nEvery effect is multiplied by the juice factor (§6.14) and by power-ups and Supernova where they apply.\n\n### 6.5 Movement and turbo\n\n- Velocity approaches `stick × runSpeed × ZOOM factor` at `accel` (900 u/s²). Facing turns toward the movement; on\n  defense with the opponent holding the ball, the defender faces the ball handler and shuffles (defensive stance).\n- Bodies are `E.Body` (radius 6, gravity `playerGravity` 900) on the arena TileMap; players push each other apart\n  (separation, no damage); `E.SpatialHash` is unnecessary at four players.\n- **Turbo** (0..1 per player): held and moving drains `turboDrain` 0.30/s; not held refills `turboRefill` 0.20/s after\n  `turboRefillDelay` 0.35 s; speed × `turboMult` 1.5, jumps × 1.15; required to start a dunk (≥ 0.05); a shove costs\n  0.25, a spin move 0.2. Supernova and Rocket Kicks change it (§6.12, §6.15).\n- A sharp change of direction at speed squeaks the sneakers and kicks dust.\n\n### 6.6 The ball\n\nStates: `held` (by its owner's hands), `dribble` (owner; position from the dribble cycle, §7), `pass` (flying to a\ntarget), `shot` (flying with its shot record: shooter, points, decided outcome, release time, perfect flag), `loose`\n(free physics), `dead` (scripted: after a score, during breaks), `inbound`.\n\n- Gravity `ballGravity` 760; floor bounce restitution `ballBounce` 0.62 with friction; rolling decays.\n- **Rim:** a torus (ring radius 9, tube 1.2). Closest point Q on the ring circle to the ball's centre; if\n  `|ball − Q| < 4.5 + 1.2` reflect the velocity along the normal with `rimBounce` 0.45 and a little friction; the rim\n  \"rattles\" (visual bend + clank). Substep the ball 4× (1/480 s) within 40 units of a rim; never let it tunnel.\n- **Backboard:** the plane x = 40 (or 600) within its y/z bounds; restitution 0.55; a thud.\n- **Arena walls:** 24 outside the lines; restitution 0.5.\n- **Basket:** the ball's centre crosses z = 80 downward within 6.75 of the rim centre: score once (a `scored` flag\n  holds until it falls below z 60); the net slows it (vz × 0.6) as it passes.\n- Players do not collide with the ball except to catch, steal, block, tip or goaltend it.\n\n### 6.7 Shooting\n\nPressing shoot with the ball (out of dunk range, or without turbo) starts a **jump shot**: gather 0.08 s, jump\n(`jumpShotVz` 250 + SWAT bonus), the ball rises above the forehead. Releasing the button releases the ball; holding past\nthe top releases it automatically 0.25 s after the top (a late release). The **release meter** (a thin arc around the","numLines":400,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1611}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01SUvGLpGFWh2Hv9PJ95ep63","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
